OBJECTIVES=[('11.1 Differentiation of function of two variables',['Define a function of two variables.','Define partial derivative.','Find partial derivatives of a function of two variables.']),('11.2 Euler’s Theorem',['Define a homogeneous function of degree n.','State and prove Euler’s theorem on homogeneous functions.','Verify Euler’s theorem for homogeneous functions of different degrees (simple cases).','Use MAPLE command diff to find partial derivatives.'])]
INTRO=[heading('Introduction'),paragraph('The goal of this unit is to extend the methods of single variable differential calculus to functions of two variables. In many practical situations, the value of one quantity may depend on the values of two or more others. For example, the amount of water in a reservoir may depend on the amount of rainfall and on the amount of water consumed by local residents. The current in an electrical circuit may vary with the electromotive force, the capacitance, the resistance, and the impedance in the circuit. The flow of blood from an artery into a small capillary may depend on the diameter of the capillary and the pressure in both the artery and the capillary. The output of a factory may depend on the amount of capital invested in the plant and on the size of the labor force. We will analyse such situations using functions of several variables.'),paragraph('In many problems involving functions of several variables, the goal is to find the derivative of the function with respect to one of its variables when all the others are held constant. In this unit, we need to develop the concept and shall see how it can be used to find slopes and rates of change in case of two variables function.')]
FUNCTION_START=[heading('11.1 Differentiation of the function of two variables'),paragraph('In the real world, physical quantities often depend on two or more variables. For example, we might be concerned with the temperature on a metal plate at various points at time t. The locations of temperature on the plate are given as ordered pairs (x,y), so that the temperature T can be considered as a function of two location variables x and y, as well as a time variable t. The notation of a function of single variable, we might extend this as T(x,y,t). We begin our study of function of two variables by examining this notation and a few other basic concepts.'),paragraph('For illustration, if a company produces x items at a cost of 10 rupees per item, then total cost C(x) of producing x items is:'),eq(r'C(x)=10x'),paragraph('The cost is a function of one independent variable, the number of items produced. If the company wants to produce two products, with x of one at 10 rupees each and y of another at 15 rupees each, the total cost is a function of two independent variables x and y:'),eq(r'C(x,y)=10x+15y'),paragraph('When x=5 and y=12, the total cost is:'),eq(r'C(5,12)=10(5)+15(12)=230\ \mathrm{rupees}'),heading('11.1.1 Function of two variables'),paragraph('A function z=f(x,y) is a function of two variables x and y, if for each given pair (x,y), we determine a single value of z. Where x,y and z are real variables.')]
FUNCTION_END=[paragraph('The real numbers x and y are independent variables; z is the dependent variable. The set of all ordered pairs of real numbers (x,y) such that f(x,y) is a real number is the domain of f, and the set of all values of f(x,y) is the range.'),heading('Example 1'),paragraph('Show z=f(x,y)=√(1−x+y) is a function of two independent variables x,y. Find its domain and range.'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('For this function, show the transformation of two independent variables (inputs) x and y gives just a single dependent variable z. For x=2,y=1:'),eq(r'z=f(2,1)=\sqrt{1-2+1}=0'),paragraph('This gives just one real value z=0, and the function is therefore declared a function of two independent variables x,y. Its domain is the set of all ordered pairs for which the square root is defined:'),eq(r'1-x+y\ge0\quad\mathrm{or}\quad y\ge x-1'),paragraph('Since z must be nonnegative, the range is all z≥0.'),heading('Remember'),paragraph('Functions of more than one independent variable are called multivariate functions.'),note('A real domain may be specified as a subset D⊆ℝ² rather than automatically being the maximal formula domain. In Example 1, the principal square root gives exactly one output for every pair with y≥x−1; checking a single input illustrates this but does not prove the function property by itself. Every z≥0 is attained, for example at (x,y)=(0,z²−1), which proves the full range.')]
PARTIAL_INTRO=[heading('11.1.2 Partial derivative'),paragraph('To give a clear concept of partial derivative, consider a real-life situation. A small firm makes two products, radios and audiocassette recorders. The profit from these is:'),eq(r'P(x,y)=40x^2-10xy+5y^2-80','i'),paragraph('Here x is the number of radios sold and y is the number of recorders sold. How do changes in x or y affect P? Suppose radio sales stay at 10 units and only recorder sales vary. Management wants the rate (marginal profit/derivative of the profit function) with which the number of recorders sold changes. Fixing x=10 reduces the two-variable function to a single-variable function:'),eq(r'P(10,y)=40(10)^2-10(10)y+5y^2-80=3920-100y+5y^2'),paragraph('This gives profit from recorder sales assuming x is fixed at 10 radios. Its ordinary derivative with respect to y is:'),eq(r'\frac{d}{dy}P(10,y)=-100+10y','ii'),paragraph('This represents the per-unit profit from y recorders. The notation d/dy P(10,y) usually stands for an ordinary derivative of a single-variable function. In this two-variable case, the rate with respect to y is a partial derivative, so replace the ordinary derivative in (ii) by ∂/∂y P(10,y):'),eq(r'\frac{∂ P}{∂ y}=\frac{∂}{∂ y}P(10,y)=P_y=-100+10y'),paragraph('Prime notation is not allowed.'),paragraph('Informally, the partial derivative ∂f(x,y)/∂x with respect to x is obtained by keeping x variable and y constant.'),note('The derivative measures marginal change in profit per additional recorder near y, not total profit per unit sold. More precisely, P_y(x,y)=−10x+10y and P_y(10,y)=−100+10y. The restricted function g(y)=P(10,y) has ordinary derivative g′(y)=P_y(10,y); prime notation is valid for g but ambiguous for the unspecialized two-variable P. Unit counts are discrete physically; differentiation treats the polynomial as a continuous approximation.')]
