EXAMPLES=[{'num':'1','title':'Function of Two Variables','question':'Show f(x,y)=√(1−x+y) is a function; find its domain and range.','steps':['Use the principal real square root, which assigns exactly one nonnegative output whenever its radicand is nonnegative.','The input restriction is 1−x+y≥0, equivalently y≥x−1.','Thus the maximal real domain is the half-plane D={(x,y)∈ℝ²:y≥x−1}, including its boundary.','For the source illustration (2,1), f(2,1)=√0=0. The formula’s single-valued square root proves the function property for every valid input.','Every output is nonnegative, so the range is contained in [0,∞).','Conversely, for any z≥0 choose x=0,y=z²−1. This pair lies in D and f(0,z²−1)=√(z²)=z.','Therefore every nonnegative value occurs and the range is exactly [0,∞).'],'answer':'Domain y≥x−1; range [0,∞).','progressiveSolution':True,'solutionOrigin':'SpaceBook learning enhancement','sourceReferences':[{'page':301,'jpg':'155_page_1.jpg','verification':'Source square-root sign, sample input and inequality checked'}]}]
